            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A multifaceted symbol primarily representing divine protection and refuge, or a typological foreshadowing of the spiritual reality found in Christ, as well as human transience or the gloom of death.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"An object or activity shaped like its casting substance that marks the reach of its covering, specifically applied to holy assemblies as the shadow of Christ's substance.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Either divine protection/covering, a typological prefiguration of Christ, human brevity, or the realm of death.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A shape mimicking its casting substance and marking its covering, conceptually uniting typological prefiguration (Christ's substance) with spatial covering (protection/reach).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book unifies the consensus categories of typological foreshadowing and protective covering under a single structural definition, while treating the 'shadow of death' as its opposite. It omits the consensus meaning of fleeting transience/mortality.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book synthesizes the disparate consensus meanings into a single coherent image rather than materially contradicting them.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book relies on the same primary biblical referents as the consensus (Colossians 2, Hebrews 8, Psalm 91) but distills them into a unified mechanism—the shape and boundary of a covering cast by a substance—rather than treating typology and protection as isolated symbolic meanings.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's unified structural definition does not seem to accommodate the prominent biblical usage of shadow to represent fleeting transience and brevity (e.g., Job 8:9, Psalm 144:4), which the consensus identifies as a distinct and common category.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
